content.text:
I fixed it with a one-line change in `lib/adapters/http.js:232`. On the server, axios no longer turns protocol-relative URLs into requests to some other host. A request like `instance.get('//google.com')` with a `baseURL` set now fails with `ERR_INVALID_URL`. Before, it quietly fetched `http://google.com/`.

```js
// before
const parsed = new URL(fullPath, 'http://localhost');
// after
const parsed = new URL(fullPath, platform.hasBrowserEnv ? platform.origin : undefined);
```

The cause was the hard-coded `'http://localhost'` fallback. Axios treats `//host` as an absolute URL, so it skips `baseURL`. Then `new URL()` filled in `http:` from that fallback, which gave a valid URL pointing at an attacker-chosen host. Now in Node there is no fallback, so a URL with no protocol is rejected. In a browser environment it still resolves against the page's address (`platform.origin`, which already existed). Only the Node path changes.

**Checks:**
- I ran the issue's case against a local `baseURL`: it now rejects with `ERR_INVALID_URL Invalid URL` instead of making the request.
- In `test/unit/adapters/http.js`, 109 tests pass and 2 fail: "should support upload rate limit" and "should support download rate limit". Both time out at 2000ms. I took my change out and re-ran them, and they fail the same way, so the failure isn't caused by this fix.

I didn't add a regression test.

